00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:03.030 align:start position:0%
 
good afternoon everybody my name is mark  mason i work for nature's reward  farms out of  salinas california  i'm a farm manager for the company  i'm also a certified crop advisor and a  pest control advisor for the company  my role within the company is i'm in  charge of all the pesticides and the  fertility which includes the water  inputs and the nitrogen  fertilizer inputs or all the fertilizer  inputs for the ranch  a little bit about the company  we're a fourth generation family farm  we farm  on about 6 000 land acres  in salinas valley  and it's two crops  so that's about 12 000 we do like 2.1  crops so it's a little over twelve  thousand crop acres  and it's year-round production  in the two different regions which would  be salinas valley  california and then also yuma arizona  region  we are vertically integrated so we've  got our  farming department as well as our  harvest  our sales and marketing  we also have within the farm we have a  fertility department for the  crops and then also we've got our pest  control department  the crops that we grow  we grow lettuce  broccoli  cauliflower  celery  strawberries um and then we have minor  crops that we grow like in dives grill  kale  some sprouts some swiss chard  so just a real quick  um  background of salinas valley so the  lettuce production in salinas valley  is a planting from mid-december to  mid-august with the harvest of mid-march  to the end of october  the depending on when you plant it's  anywhere from a 65 day  head lettuce crop to 120 days  and then when production is not during  this time frame it's in the arizona area  according to the monterey county crop  report from 2018 which is the most  recent  this is a  1.2 billion dollar production value for  lettuce and this includes  um head lettuce leaf lettuce romaine  mixed lettuce not baby green lettuce  um just the commodity type lettuces and  then the number two crop would be  broccoli for 2018. it's probably a  similar trend um  that lettuce is still number one  um so as we look at our sustainability  plan  um you know there's a couple things that  drive what we're doing as far as the  sustainability there's the county  regional and state  and federal regulations  that they require  upon us as growers  and  there are the retail outlets so the  buyers ask for  certain things  to do on the ranch possibly  or they may ask for certain things for  us  not to do um  activities  they may ask us not to do maybe  certain pesticides they don't want us to  use  um  and then you know the third thing here  is  part of our sustainability is you know  we we're doing a lot of stuff on our own  so we try to take care of the ground and  take care of the water  um  and so we're being proactive  on  on the ground water trying to take care  of the groundwater every all of the  irrigation that we have in salinas  valley is well water so it's all pumped  um  so  you know we're trying to take care of  the natural resources as well as  think ahead you know what's what's going  to be required of the regulators  or the retail outlets in five years from  now 10 years from now 20 years from now  and trying to to adopt maybe some of  those practices  ahead of schedule so non-production you  know just real quick um these are just  some of the things that we've attacked  that um  are part of a sustainability program  solar panels uh paperless farming so you  know we're using tablets instead of  uh you know electronic tablets instead  of clipboards with schedules and  um you know there's no time sheets  there's it's all uh pet tiger  as a program for time sheets  uh electronic program  variable drive irrigation wells to try  to  reduce  pg e  power um we've fenced the salinas river  to prevent trash from blowing into the  river the river drains into the pacific  ocean  so we get a lot of trash coming from  highway 101  so we've have fenced our have fenced  where our ranch borders the rivers huge  recycling program of drip tape  plastic mulch which we use for our  strawberries a huge recycling program of  all of our pesticide  containers and harvest or pesticide  cardboard boxes  and harvest cardboard  and then as well as our pesticide  plastic containers  so in our sustainability plan  you know there's a couple pesticides  that  that are targets that we're trying to  reduce we're we're definitely trying to  reduce all  pesticides but there's some  and you know neonics and pyrethroids are  kind of the poster child of of products  that need to be reduced  so one way that we're doing this is  we're using smart spray technology so  when the lettuce is small the sprayer  comes through it  only sprays  what it sees  so  there's a huge savings in products  applied  at this point  grassed waterways so  we try to grasp the  waterways  that carry storm water or possible tail  water  and a lot of times that tail water may  have  some of these pesticides in it and so  the grass waterways  are there to help filter  use kind of as filter strips to filter  out some of this some of these  pesticides to prevent them from going  into  a discharge point which will eventually  end up  possibly to the salinas river and into  the ocean  um and then our pollination our  pollinator strategy  so  uh you know we've encouraged vegetation  where we can  in non  farm areas  non-crop areas i guess i should say  and then also you know production  or or spraying at night so when we go  out and spray  we try to do most if not all of our  spraying is done in the evenings this is  also to help to help with  avoiding  non-target  drift from uh wind salinas can get windy  so  again we spray at night for that reason  also  nitrogen use efficiency um as the crops  get  uh  ready for fertilizer we will go out and  pull a soil sample  and verify that  they need fertilizer so we run it  through a computer program called crop  manage which was developed by michael  khan university of california  we will put the night the soil sample  results from a quick test into the crop  manage system makes a recommendation  and  we take that recommendation as a tool  we  can um  use it use the number that it's given us  or we can  change it  as we see fit as managers um  that's been a huge part of our lettuce  production in the last  five years i would say  high carbon compost the idea here is  that we put on high carbon compost in  the fall  and as the rains come  so it'll be high carbon compost in the  fall after  you know say a lettuce crop has been  harvested and the idea here is is that  when the rains come  the carbon has binded with the nitrogen  and when it rains it's not leached and  then in the spring  when we're ready to plant this uh  nitrogen becomes available um  number three here will be a smart side  dress  of fertilizer so it's same technology as  the smart spray  and  you know this is still in development  but the idea here is that we  go instead of making a line of  fertilizer we  put the machine in there and whenever it  sees we set the parameters of you know  when you see a  uh a lettuce plant we want to fertilize  you know two inches on each side of that  plant  and so then you're going through in your  smart side dressing  and then  to water use efficiency  we are again back to crop manage using  crop manage for irrigation  recommendations we're using it as a  we're using it as a tool  to help  manage our irrigation scheduling  and it uses et  to help make these recommendations and  then we're also using the open et  which  will  help us determine  how much water  is needed to just replace what the plant  is using so the you know the et is just  the minimum that it takes to produce the  crop you know we we definitely use water  more than the e.t in certain situations  whether it's a leaching  or soil type  but we're using this et as the starting  point  and then buried drip tape for lettuce  germination that way we avoid  wind  patterns from the sprinklers  and um  when we've got  wind patterns at 10 we tend to use a lot  more water than necessary  so  you know the wind blows constantly a lot  of times in the area so it's hard to  avoid these wind patterns and  and then crop uniformity you know this  is uh i see uniformity  and high yields as  a way to  to make us more sustainable i mean it  makes sense  to me that as your yield and your  uniformity increases and some of these  inputs  maybe stay the same  you've got you know less  less  less natural resources i guess you could  say per unit harvested  so that's going to be on this next slide  here so  um as we get  more uniform and higher yields we have  i'm going to call it a fixed input  per unit harvested so if we can get more  boxes off the same acre  with um you know every time we run a  tractor through right it's the same  amount of fuel per acre and  you know water may change a little bit  fertilizer may change a little bit as  you get these higher yields or  um  you know pesticides aren't going to  change right it's just the same amount  per acre and unless you're talking the  smart spray  so i think all of these  fixed inputs  um  kind of are reduced per unit as we get  our crops more uniform  and higher yielding  um so you know to finish off um  as  thinking for the future on uniformity um  you know mechanical harvest or and it's  gonna be destructive harvest in my  opinion  uniformity is going to be the key  and  that's going to be what we need as well  as the plant structure is going to need  to be built  to accommodate these harvesters that are  going to come in high-rise broccoli is a  good example there's some iceberg  lettuce that may sit up 2-3 inches off  the ground  um you know those are going to be  i think as we get into mechanical  harvest is are what we need and the and  then it needs to be uniform as it's  going to be destructive uh as you get  more uniform there's some harvest models  developed  by some drone companies that work pretty  well  but um you know uniformity is going to  be  is going to be key in making these work  and then as you get more uniform then  you know you've got less food loss um so  that's important also  so  um  and again you know the last point here  is going to be that as  yields increase and and the uniformity  is there it fits into our  sustainability goal  so with that um i'll wrap it up i think  i'm pretty close to on time and uh thank  you i'll be  available for questions thanks

